---
title: Scheduled Task XML Written to SYSVOL
id: cb93827c-9577-4870-9503-725eb08a0db3
name: gentlemen_sysvol_scheduledtask_write
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects write access to a ScheduledTasks.xml file in SYSVOL, the Group Policy
    Preferences mechanism ransomware operators use to run an encryptor on every
    domain-joined host. Base rule for the GPO-push correlation below.
references:
    - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/28/the-gentlemen-ransomware-dissecting-a-self-propagating-go-encryptor/
author: Dalton Hunter
date: 2026-11-03
tags:
    - attack.defense-evasion
    - attack.privilege-escalation
    - attack.t1484.001
    - attack.execution
    - attack.t1053.005
logsource:
    product: windows
    service: security
    definition: 'Requirements: Audit Detailed File Share enabled on domain controllers'
detection:
    selection:
        EventID: 5145
        ShareName|endswith: 'SYSVOL'
        RelativeTargetName|endswith: 'ScheduledTasks.xml'
        AccessList|contains: '%%4417'
    condition: selection
falsepositives:
    - Administrators creating scheduled tasks through Group Policy Preferences
level: medium

---
title: GPO Modified Then Scheduled Task Pushed via SYSVOL
id: b9273655-7d91-47de-810d-6c0734ed9167
status: experimental
description: >
    Correlates a Group Policy Object modification with a ScheduledTasks.xml write to
    SYSVOL by the same account within one hour, consistent with The Gentlemen pushing
    their encryptor domain-wide through a modified GPO.
references:
    - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/28/the-gentlemen-ransomware-dissecting-a-self-propagating-go-encryptor/
author: Dalton Hunter
date: 2026-11-03
correlation:
    type: temporal
    rules:
        - gentlemen_gpo_container_modified
        - gentlemen_sysvol_scheduledtask_write
    group-by:
        - SubjectUserName
    timespan: 1h
falsepositives:
    - Planned software deployment through Group Policy; correlate with change tickets
level: critical

